<!-- 01 — THE SITUATION -->
<section class="container measure section-block section-block--tight">
  <p class="label reveal" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">01 — The situation</p>
  <h2 class="display-md reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">One advantage, four listeners</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Whoever developed a product knows its advantages precisely — in the language of development. Between manufacturer and end customer, however, there are often several stages: purchasing, retail, workshop, planner. Each assesses the same product by its own criteria.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">An argument that convinces the engineer can leave the dealer cold. And an advantage the specialist grasps at once can mean nothing to the end customer — or even look like a disadvantage.</p>
  <p class="pull-quote reveal reveal-delay-2">A technical detail only becomes a reason to buy once someone translates what it means for each stage.</p>
</section>

<!-- 02 — WHAT IS CREATED -->
<section class="container measure section-block">
  <p class="label reveal" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">02 — What is created</p>
  <h2 class="display-md reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The right reason for every stage</h2>
  <ul class="check-list">
    {%- for item in [
      "A line of argument per stage: for engineering, purchasing, retail and end customers — from the same facts, but not in the same sentence",
